# Commit Message Rules

When generating commit messages, follow these rules:

## Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>
```

## Type Rules

- `feat`: New features
- `fix`: Bug fixes
- `refactor`: Code refactoring
- `docs`: Documentation changes
- `style`: Code style changes
- `test`: Test changes
- `chore`: Build/tooling changes

## Scope Rules

- Extract scope from branch name (part after `/`)
- Use kebab-case
- Examples: `configuration`, `employee-management`, `auth`

## Subject Rules

- Start with lowercase
- No period at the end
- Max 50 characters
- Use imperative mood

## Body Rules

- List specific changes
- Use bullet points
- Group related changes
- Include file names when relevant

## Branch Name Integration

- Always include branch name in commit message
- Format: `<branch-name>: <description>`
- Extract feature name from branch for scope

## Examples

```
feat/configuration-jd: add recruitment description update functionality

- Add new update component for recruitment descriptions
- Refactor hooks structure for better organization
- Add action buttons for table operations
- Update existing components and types
```

## AI Assistant Instructions

When user asks for commit messages:

1. Get current branch name
2. Analyze staged changes
3. Generate appropriate type and scope
4. Create descriptive subject and body
5. Follow the format above
